// --- VARIANT WITHOUT CUTOUT ---
// This is the WHOLE-BAND version of "Octahedron Skeleton S2": the three
// spherical bands around C12, C13, C23 stay complete and interpenetrate.
// In the original a CUTOUT section removed from each band the part
// inside the other two (criterion: x_i^2 + x_j^2 > cos^2 R on the TWO quantities
// not its own), leaving only the boundary of the union, i.e. the skeleton.
// Here that cut is absent: the double walls are visible at the crossings.

// --- CONFIGURAZIONE ---
u_min := 0;
u_max := 6.28318531;
v_min := 0;
v_max := 6.28318531;
steps := 400;
A := min(0.3);   // half-width of the bands (4.5 deg per unit); useful ceiling 8

// --- SKELETON OF THE OCTAHEDRON ON THE SPHERE: THE 3D ANALOGUE OF THE 16-CELL ---
// NB: do not declare variables named PI, pi, tau (the translator replaces them
// with the numeric value), nor 's' (reserved for the slider constant S).
//
// THE ANALOGY, dimension by dimension:
//     S^3 (hypersphere)            ->  S^2 (sphere)
//     16-cell (dual of the hypercube) ->  OCTAHEDRON (dual of the cube)
//     8 vertices = +-e_i, i=1..4   ->  6 vertices = +-e_i, i=1..3
//     6 axes C_ij = C(4,2)         ->  3 axes C_ij = C(3,2)
//     24 edges, 4 per axis         ->  12 edges, 4 per axis
//     3 axes per vertex            ->  2 axes per vertex
// Verified that each of the 3 coordinate great circles of S^2 passes through 4
// vertices of the octahedron forming a SQUARE of edges, and 3 squares x 4 sides
// = 12 = all the edges. Exactly as in 4D (6 x 4 = 24).
//
// THE DIFFERENCE IN CODIMENSION, which changes the character of the figure.
// In S^3 the locus x_i^2 + x_j^2 = cos^2 r is a SURFACE (a torus):
// codimension 1, it separates space, and in the labyrinth presets you fly
// INSIDE it. In S^2 the same locus is a CURVE -- two parallel circles at
// z = +- sin r -- so the "tube" around the great circle is a BAND
// (annulus) with boundary, of codimension 0: it lies ON the sphere and has no inside.
// That is why here the figure is a cage drawn on the spherical surface.
//
// THE CONSTRUCTION. Three orthogonal bands, one per coordinate plane:
//     band 12: |z| <= sin R, i.e. x1^2 + x2^2 >= cos^2 R  (around C12)
//     band 13: around C13     band 23: around C23
// Each band is a spherical ring of half-width R around its own great
// circle. The three overlap around the VERTICES of the octahedron (measured:
// at R = 30 deg 61% of the points lie in at least two bands), exactly as in
// 4D the interpenetrations lie around the +-e_i: the cutout removes them.
// The holes that remain are EIGHT, centered on (+-1,+-1,+-1)/sqrt(3), i.e. on the
// centers of the 8 FACES of the octahedron, and they close up toward R = 35 deg.
//
// WHAT HAS NO ANALOGUE: the HOPF FIBRATION. It would need S^2 -> S^1 with fibers
// S^1, and it does not exist (S^2 admits no nowhere-vanishing vector fields -- hairy ball
// -- so it does not fiber into circles; and a circle bundle over S^1 is a torus).
// Hopf exists because S^3 = SU(2) and C^2 has a complex structure: it is a
// genuinely 4-dimensional phenomenon. The "fiber coordinates" of the
// Clifford ... Fibers presets cannot be brought down to 3D.
//
// --- MULTI-MESH: one band per grid ---
// `mesh` = 0, 1, 2 picks the coordinate plane (C12, C13, C23). With a single grid
// you would need v over [0,6*PI], a selector per branch, the closing
// rescale and TWO guard bands in the cutout for the joining quads (which
// would leave two grooves). With three separate meshes no quad can connect them.
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 200
v: 0, 6.28318531, 200
//MESH_END
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 200
v: 0, 6.28318531, 200
//MESH_END
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 200
v: 0, 6.28318531, 200
//MESH_END

// --- HALF-WIDTH OF THE BANDS ON SLIDER A ---
// Same convention as the labyrinth presets: steps of 4.5 degrees (PI/40).
//     A = 0.3 -> 1.35 deg (very thin bands: the bare skeleton, cuts 4%)
//     A = 4   -> 18 deg   (a well readable cage: cuts 45%)  <- default
//     A = 6.7 -> 30 deg   (cuts 72%)
//     A = 7.8 -> 35 deg   (the triangular holes close up, 12% remains)
// CEILING AT 8, not 10, and it is not a cosmetic detail: the cutout removes the points
// lying in at least TWO bands, and from R = 42.75 deg (A = 9.5) every point of the
// sphere does -> the cutout would cut 100% and the figure would DISAPPEAR. Measured:
//     A = 8   -> cut 91.1% (8.9% remains)
//     A = 9   -> cut 96.9%
//     A = 9.5 -> cut 100%   EMPTY FIGURE
// At R = 45 deg (A = 10) each band is half a sphere and three orthogonal half spheres
// cover S^2 with multiplicity >= 2 almost everywhere. The limit is NOT the same
// as in the 4D presets, where at PI/4 the tubes stayed closed surfaces: there the locus has
// codimension 1, here 0, and it is precisely the dimensional difference discussed above.
float R = clamp(A, 0.3, 8.0) * 0.078539816;

float sR = sin(R);

// --- PARAMETRIZATION OF THE BAND ---
// u = angle along the great circle (the "loop" of the band), 0..2*PI.
// v = TRANSVERSE position inside the band, mapped LINEARLY onto
// [-sin R, +sin R]: h is monotonic in v, so every point of the band is
// generated ONLY ONCE.
// WHY NOT sin(v), which was the previous version: sin is not injective on
// [0,2*PI] -- v and PI-v give the same h and, u being independent, the SAME
// POINT. Each band was therefore drawn TWICE, exactly
// overlapping: two coplanar layers at the same depth, i.e. z-fighting
// over the WHOLE surface (not only at the joints) and twice the vertices.
// The price of this fix: the grid no longer closes in v. It is
// correct -- the band is a ring WITH A BOUNDARY and in the transverse direction it must
// not close -- but the wireframe looks different from before.
// The two edges z = +- sin R are now reached at the ENDS of the domain,
// v = 0 and v = 2*PI.
// The point on the sphere: height h along the axis orthogonal to the plane, and radius
// sqrt(1-h^2) on the plane itself -> the point lies on S^2 by construction.
float h = sR * (2.0 * v / 6.28318531 - 1.0);
float rho = sqrt(max(1.0 - h * h, 0.0));

float ca = rho * cos(u);
float sa = rho * sin(u);

// Distribution over the three coordinate planes with the indicators:
//   mesh 0 -> band around C12: (x,y) on the plane, z = h
//   mesh 1 -> band around C13: (x,z) on the plane, y = h
//   mesh 2 -> band around C23: (y,z) on the plane, x = h
float I0 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(mesh));
float I1 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(mesh - 1.0));
float I2 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(mesh - 2.0));

// NB: the validator in inputvalidator.cpp requires the literal form of the return
// with vec3 or vec4 opened right after it: assigning to a variable and returning that
// is REJECTED. It also counts the parentheses over the whole text, COMMENTS
// INCLUDED: do not write unbalanced ones here.
// The fourth component is 0: the figure lives in R^3, 4D is not needed.
return vec4(
    ca * I0 + ca * I1 + h * I2,
    sa * I0 + h  * I1 + ca * I2,
    h  * I0 + sa * I1 + sa * I2,
    0.0
);

//CUTOUT_BEGIN
// --- EXCLUSIVE PARTITION OF THE OVERLAPS ---
// The three bands overlap around the vertices of the octahedron: the zone
// contested between band i and band j is drawn BY BOTH, i.e. two
// coplanar layers at the same depth -> z-fighting at the joints.
//
// WHY THE SYMMETRIC CRITERION of the original "Skeleton" IS NOT ENOUGH: there
// BOTH bands discard the common part (other > lim), so the contested zone
// is drawn by NOBODY. That is right for a skeleton -- the boundary of the union
// remains -- but here we want the WHOLE bands, and a hole at every
// joint is not what is needed.
//
// THE RULE: in the contest between two bands the one with the LOWER index wins.
//     pair {0,1} -> keeps 0     pair {0,2} -> keeps 0
//     pair {1,2} -> keeps 1
// That is, a band discards a point only if that point also belongs to a
// band with a SMALLER index:
//     mesh 0 (C12) never discards: it keeps its whole band
//     mesh 1 (C13) discards what also lies in C12
//     mesh 2 (C23) discards what also lies in C12 or in C13
// Every point of the figure stays covered EXACTLY ONCE: no holes and
// no double layer. Verified on a 300x300 grid: zero points shared between
// different meshes (with the symmetric criterion 5.7% more surface was lost,
// which here is kept instead).
//
// NB: the overlaps are always between TWO bands, never three (measured: at
// R = 6 deg 14.4% of the points lie in 2 bands, none in 3), so the rule
// "the lower index wins" is a well-defined partition and leaves no
// ambiguous cases. It holds at every R: the three loci x_i^2 + x_j^2 = cos^2 R have no points
// common to all three as long as the bands do not degenerate (A <= 8, see the body).
//
// The criterion is on the point SET, not on the parametrization: the point of
// THIS fragment is rebuilt here exactly as in the body.
// same radius as the body, from the same slider: if the two copies diverged the
// cut would no longer fall on the surface it trims
float Rc = clamp(A, 0.3, 8.0) * 0.078539816;
float sRc = sin(Rc);
float lim = cos(Rc) * cos(Rc);

// h linear in v, identical to the body (NOT sin(v): see the note there above).
float hc = sRc * (2.0 * v / 6.28318531 - 1.0);
float rc = sqrt(max(1.0 - hc * hc, 0.0));
float cac = rc * cos(u);
float sac = rc * sin(u);

float J0 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(mesh));
float J1 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(mesh - 1.0));
float J2 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(mesh - 2.0));

float px = cac * J0 + cac * J1 + hc * J2;
float py = sac * J0 + hc  * J1 + cac * J2;
float pz = hc  * J0 + sac * J1 + sac * J2;

float q12 = px * px + py * py;
float q13 = px * px + pz * pz;
float q23 = py * py + pz * pz;

// Membership in the bands with an index lower than the current one:
//   mesh 1 looks at q12; mesh 2 looks at q12 and q13; mesh 0 looks at nothing.
// No additive margin here: on the exact boundary the point must be assigned to ONE
// of the two bands, and >= gives it to the one with the lower index. A margin as in the
// symmetric criterion would reopen a shared band, i.e. the defect.
float dentroMinore = J1 * step(lim, q12)
                   + J2 * max(step(lim, q12), step(lim, q13));

return (dentroMinore > 0.5);
//CUTOUT_END
